  "raw": {
    "https://ntu.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/NTU-Main-Campus-Singapore/Research-Assistant--Learning-Sciences-and-Assessment-Department---NIE-_R00025683": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Learning Sciences and Assessment Department) [NIE]",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe National Institute of Education invites suitable applications for the position of Research Assistant on a 24-month contract at the Learning Sciences and Assessment Department . Project Title: Curiosity, 21st Century Competencies, and Self-Directed Learning Project Introduction: The project aims to understand how curiosity and 21st century competencies develop in self-directed, student-initiated learning experiences in MOE secondary schools. The project uses a variety of methods such as surveys, digital device use, and generative AI chatbots. Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in Psychology, Education, or a related field Proficiency in quantitative research, such as survey data and analysis Preferably one year of research experience (fresh graduates are welcome to apply) Experience working with MOE schools Experience using AI tools to support research activities Responsibilities: Conduct literature review Liaise with schools and research partners Manage data collection, data cleaning, and data analysis Assist in the preparation of project reports and presentations Support project administration, including meetings, documentation, and any other duties as assigned by the Principal Investigator Application Applicants (external and internal) will apply via Workday. We regret that only shortlisted candidates will be notified. Closing Date Closing date for advertisements will be set to 14 calendar days from date of posting. Hiring Institution: NIE"
    },
    "https://ntu.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/NTU-Main-Campus-Singapore/Research-Assistant--Building-a-Local-AI-Personalized-assistant-for-Hand-Drawn-Animation-Artist-with-ComfyUI-_R00025674": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Building a Local AI Personalized assistant for Hand Drawn Animation Artist with ComfyUI)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSchool of Art, Design and Media (ADM) is a premier creative hub in Singapore, renowned for combining art, technology, and design. We are seeking a Research Assistant to work on an exciting project using ComfyUI to build a personalized, local AI assistant for a hand-drawn animation artist. You will support world-renowned animator Professor Raimund Krumme during his two-month residency at ADM (Mid Sept–Mid Nov 2026), helping to bring his strictly analogue, ink-and-brush process into a local, privacy-safe AI workflow — one that is genuinely simple for a non-technical artist to pick up and use. Key Responsibilities: Set up and optimize local, open-source AI models (Stable Diffusion/ComfyUI) on high-end workstation hardware, with no cloud dependency. Train local LoRAs and ControlNets on a curated archive of the artist's analogue sketches and scans. Package the ComfyUI workflow into a simple, easy-to-run form (e.g. presets, node groups, or a lightweight Gradio front-end) that the artist can operate independently. Provide on-the-ground technical support during Prof. Krumme's residency (14 Sept – 13 Nov 2026). Document the system architecture and user-testing feedback for the final research report. Job Requirements: Bachelor's degree in Animation, Film Studies, Media Studies or a related discipline. Hands-on experience with ComfyUI and local AI deployments (Automatic1111, Forge, etc.), including training LoRAs/ControlNets. Understanding of local hardware optimization (GPU/VRAM management). Comfortable putting together a simple working interface for a workflow (e.g. Gradio) — no formal UX/UI design background required. Able to translate an artist's creative intent into technical parameters, with patience for working with a non-technical collaborator. We regret to inform that only shortlisted candidates will be notified. Hiring Institution: NTU"
    },
    "https://ntu.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/NTU-Main-Campus-Singapore/Research-Assistant--Asian-Languages---Cultures-Department---NIE-_R00025685": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Asian Languages & Cultures Department) [NIE]",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe National Institute of Education invites suitable applications for the position of Research Assistant on a 7-month contract at the Asian Languages & Cultures Department . Project Title: Preserving the Beat: Rebana Singapura as Intangible Heritage and a Tool for Social Cohesion Project Introduction: This project investigates the historical evolution, cultural significance, and contemporary role of Rebana Singapura, a uniquely Singaporean adaptation of traditional Malay percussion. Emerging from the innovations of local musicians, Rebana Singapura reflects the dynamic interaction of Nusantara drumming practices with Singapore’s own multicultural landscape. Despite its growing presence in schools, community events, and professional ensembles, Rebana Singapura remains under-documented in both scholarship and the national heritage archives. This study seeks to fill that gap by creating a comprehensive record of its origins, craftsmanship, performance practices, and social role. While the rebana is rooted in Malay tradition, its performance contexts, whether at multicultural festivals, school programmes, or community workshops, have positioned it as a cultural bridge accessible to wider Singaporean society. This research will therefore not only preserve Rebana Singapura as intangible cultural heritage but also examine its relevance as a tool for fostering social cohesion across communities. Requirements: Bachelor’s Degree in Malay Studies or a related field. Prior experience in research data analysis, particularly qualitative research, will be advantageous. Strong interest in Malay cultural heritage, traditional performing arts, or community-based cultural research. Familiarity with qualitative research methods and analysis, including coding, thematic analysis and interpretation of interview, survey and observational data. Proficiency in English and Malay language is required to analyse and interpret research data and materials in both languages. Proficiency in organising, cleaning, coding and managing qualitative data, including transcripts and other research materials. Experience with qualitative data analysis software such as NVivo, or the willingness and ability to learn, would be an advantage. Ability to synthesise research findings, identify key themes and patterns, and contribute to the preparation of research reports, presentations and publications. Proficiency of digital tools for data management, documentation and research coordination. Meticulous, organised, with the ability to work independently and manage substantial volumes of research data. Strong analytical and critical-thinking skills, with attention to detail and accuracy. Ability to work systematically with confidential or sensitive research materials. Strong written communication skills, with the ability to present research findings clearly and coherently. Demonstrated interest in heritage preservation, cultural research, and multicultural engagement. Responsibilities: Support the Principal Investigator in the organisation, management and analysis of research data. Review and organise interview transcripts, survey responses, field notes and other research materials to ensure that the dataset is complete and systematically documented. Undertake coding and thematic analysis of qualitative data, including identifying key themes, patterns and relationships across the dataset. Assist with the analysis and synthesis of findings from different data sources and contribute to the interpretation of the research findings. Conduct targeted archival or literature research, where necessary, to support the analysis and contextualisation of the findings. Maintain systematic records of research materials and analytical outputs, ensuring compliance with relevant research ethics, data management and archival requirements. Assist the Principal Investigator in the preparation of research reports, archival submissions, presentations, publications and other dissemination materials. Undertake other research-related tasks pertaining to data analysis, documentation and reporting as required by the Principal Investigator. Any other administrative duties as assigned by the Principal Investigator. Application Applicants (external and internal) will apply via Workday. We regret that only shortlisted candidates will be notified. Closing Date Closing date for advertisements will be set to 14 calendar days from date of posting. Hiring Institution: NIE"
    },
    "https://ntu.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/NTU-Main-Campus-Singapore/Research-Associate--Environmental-Engineering-_R00025681": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Environmental Engineering)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe School of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) at Nanyang Technological University is a leading school for Sustainable Built Environment. It has over 60 faculty members and more than 1,500 students and is rapidly expanding its range of education and research activities. We are looking for a Research Associate to conduct life-cycle assessment on solid waste treatment and management systems. Key Responsibilities: Design and implement experimental studies on solid waste treatment processes Integrate experimental findings with quantitative modeling and life-cycle assessment (LCA) to assess system performance and environmental impacts. Job Requirements: Master’s degree in Environmental Engineering or related fields At least 1 year of relevant experiences in life-cycle assessment Familiarity with related literature and software Good written and oral communication skills Entry level candidates are welcome to apply We regret to inform that only shortlisted candidates will be notified Hiring Institution: NTU"
    },
    "https://ntu.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/NTU-Main-Campus-Singapore/Research-Fellow--Natural-Sciences-and-Science-Education-Department---NIE-_R00025575": {
      "title": "Research Fellow (Natural Sciences and Science Education Department) [NIE]",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe National Institute of Education invites suitable applications for the position of Research Fellow at the Natural Sciences and Science Education Department . Project Title: Intelligent Single Pixel Opto-Spintronic Terahertz Imaging (iSPOT) Project Introduction: Terahertz (THz) frequencies represent a frontier with vast potential in non-destructive imaging. However, its application for high-resolution imaging at the micron scale remains hindered by limitations imposed by its sub-millimeter wavelengths [1 THz ~ 300 µm]. This necessitates the need to transcend the diffraction limit of THz waves to unlock imaging capabilities at both surface and sub-surface levels. Recently, computationally intensive THz imaging methods have emerged, leveraging single-pixel detection and spatially patterned THz waves. Generating spatially patterned THz waves typically involves a two-step process: (i) photoexcitation of silicon with patterned laser pulses, leading to conducting (illuminated) and non-conducting (non-illuminated) regions, and (ii) selective reflection/transmission of THz waves from the patterned photoexcited silicon surface, resulting in patterned THz radiation. However, due to the drift current from photoexcited to non-excited regions, the laser-irradiated pattern on silicon manifests blurred transition boundaries and eventually forming distorted THz patterns that introduce errors during the computation and, hence limiting the spatial resolution. Proposed Solution: Spintronic THz emitters, comprising ultrathin ferromagnet/heavy metal (FM/HM) heterostructure, have recently emerged as an efficient solution for generating broadband THz waves through femtosecond laser-induced spin excitation and inverse spin Hall effect. This ultrathin spintronic THz emitter design is hypothesized to prevent drift current between the photoexcited and non-photo-excited regions (activated by spatially patterned incident laser pulse), ensuring sharper transitions between \"On\" and \"Off\" states minimizing the THz image distortion. Overall aim: of our project is to achieve intelligent single-pixel-based THz imaging with image resolutions <20 µm (~λ/15) using spatially configurable patterned THz radiation obtained using an emerging concept of spintronic THz source activated selectively in an arbitrary spatial pattern using spatial light modulators. Methodology: involves working on three integrated verticals: (i) Optimization of FM/HM heterostructure, through detailed characterization of spin transport properties, for the realization of high-efficiency broadband spintronic THz emitter for compact THz imaging platform. (ii) Inventing a state-of-the-art single-pixel detector-based THz imaging platform aided by computational image construction methods and active polarization state manipulation of THz waves for THz imaging of metamaterial surfaces. (iii) Optimize and enhance the computational algorithm by introducing a patterned spintronic THz emitter array to resolve the two-dimensional object image from the data set in real-time. Requirements: Essential PhD in Physics, Engineering or a related field. Proven experience in THz Spectroscopy, spintronics and material science. Proficiency in optical setup alignment and material characterization. Proven experience in development, operation and optimization of THz sources and detection. Experience in THz Imaging. Proficiency in Python programming to develop codes for post-data processing. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated writing proficiency, with experience producing research papers and research reports. Preferred Strong knowledge of laboratory safety practices and procedures. Excellent organizational skills. Ability to work within given deadlines with strong attention to detail. Ability to work independently with minimum supervision. Ability to assist in the supervision of FYP / Masters / Ph.D. candidates. Responsibilities: Contribute and support the fabrication of a spintronic THz spectrometer. Design of the pump-probe optical pathway based on the oscillator laser, emission via the spintronic THz emitter, and detection using a photoconductive antenna. Development of computational algorithms to achieve high-resolution THz imaging. Prepare, write, and contribute to research reports and papers. Any other duties as assigned by the Principal Investigator. Application Applicants (external and internal) will apply via Workday. We regret that only shortlisted candidates will be notified. Closing Date Closing date for advertisements will be set to 14 calendar days from date of posting. Hiring Institution: NIE"
    },
    "https://ntu.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/NTU-Main-Campus-Singapore/Research-Fellow--Natural-Sciences-and-Science-Education-Department---NIE-_R00025576": {
      "title": "Research Fellow (Natural Sciences and Science Education Department) [NIE]",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe National Institute of Education invites suitable applications for the position of Research Fellow at the Natural Sciences and Science Education Department . Project Title: Intelligent Single Pixel Opto-Spintronic Terahertz Imaging (iSPOT) Project Introduction: Terahertz (THz) frequencies represent a frontier with vast potential in non-destructive imaging. However, its application for high-resolution imaging at the micron scale remains hindered by limitations imposed by its sub-millimeter wavelengths [1 THz ~ 300 µm]. This necessitates the need to transcend the diffraction limit of THz waves to unlock imaging capabilities at both surface and sub-surface levels. Recently, computationally intensive THz imaging methods have emerged, leveraging single-pixel detection and spatially patterned THz waves. Generating spatially patterned THz waves typically involves a two-step process: (i) photoexcitation of silicon with patterned laser pulses, leading to conducting (illuminated) and non-conducting (non-illuminated) regions, and (ii) selective reflection/transmission of THz waves from the patterned photoexcited silicon surface, resulting in patterned THz radiation. However, due to the drift current from photoexcited to non-excited regions, the laser-irradiated pattern on silicon manifests blurred transition boundaries and eventually forming distorted THz patterns that introduce errors during the computation and, hence limiting the spatial resolution. Proposed Solution: Spintronic THz emitters, comprising ultrathin ferromagnet/heavy metal (FM/HM) heterostructure, have recently emerged as an efficient solution for generating broadband THz waves through femtosecond laser-induced spin excitation and inverse spin Hall effect. This ultrathin spintronic THz emitter design is hypothesized to prevent drift current between the photoexcited and non-photo-excited regions (activated by spatially patterned incident laser pulse), ensuring sharper transitions between \"On\" and \"Off\" states minimizing the THz image distortion. Overall aim: of our project is to achieve intelligent single-pixel-based THz imaging with image resolutions <20 µm (~λ/15) using spatially configurable patterned THz radiation obtained using an emerging concept of spintronic THz source activated selectively in an arbitrary spatial pattern using spatial light modulators. Methodology: involves working on three integrated verticals: (i) Optimization of FM/HM heterostructure, through detailed characterization of spin transport properties, for the realization of high-efficiency broadband spintronic THz emitter for compact THz imaging platform. (ii) Inventing a state-of-the-art single-pixel detector-based THz imaging platform aided by computational image construction methods and active polarization state manipulation of THz waves for THz imaging of metamaterial surfaces. (iii) Optimize and enhance the computational algorithm by introducing a patterned spintronic THz emitter array to resolve the two-dimensional object image from the data set in real-time. Requirements: Essential PhD in Physics, Engineering or a related field. Proven experience in THz Spectroscopy, spintronics and material science. Proficiency in optical setup alignment and material characterization. Proven experience in development, operation and optimization of THz sources and detection. Experience in THz Imaging. Proficiency in Python programming to develop codes for post-data processing. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated writing proficiency, with experience producing research papers and research reports. Preferred Strong knowledge of laboratory safety practices and procedures. Excellent organizational skills. Ability to work within given deadlines with strong attention to detail. Ability to work independently with minimum supervision. Ability to assist in the supervision of FYP / Masters / Ph.D. candidates. Responsibilities: Contribute and support the fabrication of a spintronic THz spectrometer. Design of the pump-probe optical pathway based on the oscillator laser, emission via the spintronic THz emitter, and detection using a photoconductive antenna. Development of computational algorithms to achieve high-resolution THz imaging. Prepare, write, and contribute to research reports and papers. Any other duties as assigned by the Principal Investigator. Application Applicants (external and internal) will apply via Workday. We regret that only shortlisted candidates will be notified. Closing Date Closing date for advertisements will be set to 14 calendar days from date of posting. Hiring Institution: NIE"
    },
    "https://ntu.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/NTU-Main-Campus-Singapore/Part-Time-Lecturer-in-Chinese-Linguistics--SOH-_R00025679": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer in Chinese Linguistics (SOH)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe School of Humanities at NTU invites applications for part-time teaching of courses in the Department of Chinese in the field of Linguistics (and/or any sub-disciplines in Linguistics and Languages, including but not limited to Chinese Pragmatics, Discourse Analysis, Language Typology, Language Acquisition, Sociolinguistics etc.). For each course, the instructor will be responsible for leading classes as well as planning the syllabus, class preparation, devising and marking assignments, and handling student consultations. The course is to be taught in Chinese. Candidates should have an advanced degree and a proven track-record in teaching. That is, candidates will only be considered if they have the following: Master’s degree or higher Teaching experience at a tertiary level Applications should include the following: CV Evidence of teaching excellence if any (e.g., teaching evaluations or reference letters) Only shortlisted candidates will be notified​.  Hiring Institution: NTU"
    },
